{"slug":"cantor-set-string","title":"A Pattern That Contains Itself","topic":"backtracking","difficulty":"easy","order":406,"tags":["recursion","strings","fractals"],"timeLimitMs":2000,"concept":{"title":"The big picture is made of two smaller pictures","body":["Some patterns are defined by themselves. The **Cantor set** is drawn like this: start with one block `#`. To get the next picture, take the previous one, then leave a gap of the **same width**, then copy the previous one again. Level 0 is `#`, level 1 is `#.#`, level 2 is `#.#...#.#`.",{"code":"def cantor(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"#\"                  # the smallest picture\n    left = cantor(n - 1)\n    return left + \".\" * len(left) + left   # picture, gap of the same width, picture"},"The function builds the picture for `n` from the picture for `n - 1`. Notice that it calls `cantor(n - 1)` **once** and uses the result twice; calling it twice would do twice the work in every level. The length grows as 3^n, so level 9 already has 19683 characters."]},"statement":["Level 0 of a picture is the single character `#`. Level `k` is level `k - 1`, followed by a row of `.` characters whose length is the length of level `k - 1`, followed by level `k - 1` again. Print level `n`."],"inputFormat":["The only line has `n`."],"outputFormat":["Print the picture of level `n` on one line."],"constraints":["0 <= n <= 9"],"examples":[{"input":"2\n","output":"#.#...#.#\n","note":"Level 1 is #.# (length 3), so level 2 is #.#, then three dots, then #.# again: #.#...#.#."}],"hints":["Level 0 is the base case: a single #.","Build level n from level n - 1: copy, gap, copy.","Compute the smaller level once and reuse it for both copies."],"starter":{"python":"n = int(input())\n\n# Your code here\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n\n    // Your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n\n        // Your code here\n    }\n}\n"},"solution":{"explanation":["Level 0 is `#`. Every other level is the previous level, a gap of the same length, and the previous level again; the previous level is computed once per call."],"complexity":"Time O(3^n), space O(3^n).","code":{"python":"def cantor(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"#\"\n    left = cantor(n - 1)\n    return left + \".\" * len(left) + left\n\nn = int(input())\n\nprint(cantor(n))\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nstring cantor(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return \"#\";\n    string left = cantor(n - 1);\n    return left + string(left.size(), '.') + left;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n\n    cout << cantor((int)n) << \"\\n\";\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static String cantor(int n) {\n        if (n == 0) return \"#\";\n        String left = cantor(n - 1);\n        return left + \".\".repeat(left.length()) + left;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n\n        System.out.println(cantor((int) n));\n    }\n}\n"}},"tests":[{"input":"0\n","output":"#\n"},{"input":"1\n","output":"#.#\n"},{"input":"2\n","output":"#.#...#.#\n"},{"input":"3\n","output":"#.#...#.#.........#.#...#.#\n"},{"input":"4\n","output":"#.#...#.#.........#.#...#.#...........................#.#...#.#.........#.#...#.#\n"},{"input":"5\n","output":"#.#...#.#.........#.#...#.#...........................#.#...#.#.........#.#...#.#.................................................................................#.#...#.#.........#.#...#.#...........................#.#...#.#.........#.#...#.#\n"},{"input":"6\n","output":"#.#...#.#.........#.#...#.#...........................#.#...#.#......